</ul>

<h3>6.2 Single-Operator Risk</h3>
<p>One operator — <strong>SpaceX's Starlink</strong> — controls <strong>{starlink_pct}% of all active satellites</strong>. This unprecedented concentration has several implications:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Statistical skew:</strong> Any aggregate analysis of the satellite population (orbital regimes, inclinations, growth rates) is dominated by one constellation. Analysts should report both "all satellites" and "excluding Starlink" figures.</li>
<li><strong>Regulatory dependency:</strong> Policy decisions on orbital slots, debris mitigation, and spectrum use are effectively driven by one operator's architecture.</li>
<li><strong>Resilience:</strong> A single design flaw, supply-chain disruption, or operator decision could affect a large fraction of the global active fleet.</li>
</ul>

<h3>6.3 Launch Cadence Acceleration</h3>
<p>The growth curve shows <strong>exponential acceleration since 2020</strong>. With <strong>{since_2020:,} satellites ({since_2020_pct}% of the fleet) launched since 2020</strong>, the orbital population has multipled rapidly. If current deployment rates continue, the active satellite count could reach 25,000–30,000 within 2–3 years, driven primarily by Starlink Gen2 and competing mega-constellations (OneWeb, Project Kuiper).</p>

<h3>6.4 Policy Implications</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Licensing frameworks</strong> must evolve to handle mega-constellation applications at scale, including batch processing and automated conjunction assessment.</li>
<li><strong>Debris mitigation rules</strong> (e.g., 5-year deorbit rule) become more critical as the probability of on-orbit collisions grows with population density.</li>
